    {
      "turn": 2,
      "doctor_question": "Were you having this same kind of distractibility and careless mistakes back when you were a child or teenager?",
      "targeted_symptom": "additional_requirement_1",
      "patient_response": "Oh, definitely. Like, even way back in elementary school, my teachers were constantly having to move my desk around. I was just always out of my seat, or I'd be blurting out answers, or bothering my classmates before I even realized I was doing it. \n\nI remember Mrs. Gable in third grade—she was the one who finally moved my desk right up next to hers. She had this massive collection of ceramic frogs on her desk. Like, an absurd amount of frogs. I think someone gave her one once, and then everyone just assumed she was the \"frog lady,\" so she got them for every holiday. You know how people do that? They latch onto one thing you like and then that's your whole identity for gift-giving. My mom had the exact same thing happen with sunflowers. Our whole kitchen growing up was just covered in sunflower wallpaper, sunflower oven mitts, sunflower plates... I actually can't even look at sunflowers now without feeling a little claustrophobic. I actually shot a wedding last year at a sunflower farm up in Temecula, and the pollen was just an absolute nightmare for my lenses. I spent like three hours cleaning my gear the next day. \n\nBut wait, what were we talking about? Oh, right, being a kid. Yeah, I was always getting moved because I couldn't just sit still and be quiet.",
